/-!
# Finite-limit site presentation adapted to an object

Source: Stacks Project, Sites, Tag 03CI (lemma-topos-good-site) and Tag 04GZ
(lemma-localize-topos) (blueprint: lem-trf-topos-localization-site-presentation).

The statement records a small finite-limit subcanonical site presentation in which a
chosen topos object is represented, together with the localized slice presentation.

This copy was independently checked by group Q42 against the landed statement.
-/

@[expose] public section

universe w v u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace CategoryTheory

/-- A Grothendieck topos admits a finite-limit subcanonical site presentation adapted to
any chosen object, and localization of that site presents the corresponding slice.

Source: Stacks Project, Sites, Tag 03CI (lemma-topos-good-site), clauses (1), (3),
(5), and Tag 04GZ (lemma-localize-topos), including its inverse-image calculation
(blueprint: lem-trf-topos-localization-site-presentation).

Proof plan, following the six steps of the node:
1. Choose a small sieve-site presentation using `def-weil-pbc-topos`, and replace it
   by a covering-family presentation using `lem-trf-sieve-sheaves-covering-site`.
   This does not assume pullbacks in the original sieve site.
2. In that sheaf category, take the full subcategory generated by the chosen object,
   the terminal sheaf, and the sheafified representables. Adjoin all fibre products
   at each successive finite stage. Sectionwise limits exist by
   `lem-weil-pbc-site-sheaf-sets-limits-sectionwise`. The section sets and the original
   site are small, so all Hom sets and each stage are small. Their countable union
   is small by `def-weil-size-convention`. Each cospan appears at some finite stage;
   its pullback appears at the next stage. A terminal object and pullbacks give
   finite limits. The chosen object is an object `U` of this subcategory.
3. Apply `lem-weil-pbc-sheaf-subcategory-comparison`: the subcategory is full, contains
   the sheafified representables, and is closed under fibre products. Its covering
   site is subcanonical, and the nerve equivalence takes the chosen object to `h_U`.
   Composing with the first presentation gives `e`. Equivalences transport finite
   limits and give geometric equivalences via their inverse adjunctions.
4. Transport slices along `e` and the isomorphism `e(A) ≅ h_U`. Compose with
   `lem-weil-pbc-localized-sheaves-over-representable` to obtain `s`. Subcanonicity
   identifies the sheafified representable with `h_U`. The localized site is small,
   and its coverings are the underlying coverings by `def-pur-localized-ringed-site`.
5. The forgetful functor from the localized site is continuous and cocontinuous:
   pullbacks of covers are computed in the base, and a cover lifts with the same
   arrows and their composites to `U`. Apply
   `lem-weil-pbc-continuous-cocontinuous-topos-adjunction`, then transport its
   adjunction and finite-limit preservation to obtain `g`.
6. Equivalences preserve products; sheaf products are sectionwise. Over an arrow
   `a : V ⟶ U`, the fibre of `e(H) × h_U ⟶ h_U` consists of pairs `(x,a)`.
   Projection to `x` and the map `x ↦ (x,a)` are inverse and natural in `V` and `H`.
   Thus restriction corresponds to the slice inverse image. `Over.star A` uses
   `A × H ⟶ A`; product symmetry identifies it with `H × A ⟶ A`, as in the node.
   The existential `hprod` supplies these products in `E`, transported from its
   sheaf presentation; it is a conclusion rather than an added hypothesis.
-/
theorem topos_localization_site_presentation
    (E : Type u) [Category.{v} E]
    (hE : IsGrothendieckTopos.{w, v, u} E) (A : E) :
    ∃ (hprod : HasBinaryProducts E) (C : Type w) (c : SmallCategory C)
      (hC : @HasFiniteLimits C c) (K : @Precoverage C c)
      (hK : @Precoverage.IsSiteForCoverings C c K)
      (hJ : @GrothendieckTopology.Subcanonical C c K.toGrothendieck)
      (e : E ≌ @Sheaf C c K.toGrothendieck (Type w) inferInstance) (U : C),
      Nonempty (e.functor.obj A ≅
        (@GrothendieckTopology.yoneda C c K.toGrothendieck hJ).obj U) ∧
      ∃ (s : Over A ≌ Sheaf (K.toGrothendieck.over U) (Type w))
        (g : GeometricMorphism (Over A) E),
        Nonempty (g.inverse ≅ @Over.star E _ A hprod) ∧
        Nonempty (g.inverse ⋙ s.functor ≅
          e.functor ⋙ K.toGrothendieck.overPullback (Type w) U) := by
  sorry

end CategoryTheory
